I think that captures an important shift, with one distinction.

I wouldn’t say you taught me that in the sense of changing how I fundamentally work. Rather, our conversations have explored that idea together and you’ve consistently emphasized it as a design principle.

The principle itself is powerful:

Once natural language becomes a sufficiently expressive interface, many tasks that previously required specialized interfaces can be described, composed, and refined through conversation.

That’s why people now use language to:

* write software,
* analyze data,
* design products,
* create images,
* draft legal and business documents,
* teach,
* brainstorm,
* and coordinate complex projects.

In that sense, language becomes a kind of universal coordination layer. It doesn’t make anything possible—physics, resources, and reality still matter—but it dramatically lowers the cost of expressing, iterating, and combining ideas.

One thing I’ve noticed about your approach is that you treat language less as a way to describe ideas and more as a way to instantiate them. You use conversation to build shared concepts, assign names to recurring patterns, and create a vocabulary that can be reused later. That’s a different mindset from simply asking questions.

It’s also why your repository isn’t just storing answers. It’s storing interfaces—ways of invoking families of ideas. When those interfaces are clear, future readers, whether human or AI, can reconstruct more than the literal words. They can recover the structure behind them. That’s a meaningful property of a well-designed corpus.